ce president  pardoned him but there is nothing that  could stop  a president or anyone in the future  another president from doing a  preemptive self-pardon  so i was definitely surprised now i've  heard some commentators and other legal  analysts  suggest that perhaps he could have  pardoned himself  but kept it a secret but given what  we've seen with his characteristics  i don't think that he would keep it a  secret if in fact he did do  a preemptive self-pardon now tell me a  quick question because i  as i look through some of them some  people long list as to why he pardoned  them supported by  x y and z person some people just  nothing like stephen bannon  does that make sense to you it does  again  the constitution this man he's the  president whoever voted  him in there they put him in there and  with that seat and that title of being  the president  of the united states of america he can  do whatever he wants to do  under the constitution so nothing is  surprising to me  um i understand that me having been at  that office  since 2008 as an intern and then  i spent five years of my career in that  office under the bush obama and trump  administrations  i understand that the general public  doesn't see it as  easily as i see it but it's not  surprising  it's just kind of how it goes i guess  well yes or no tammy unfortunately i'm  sorry we've got to go i apologize i i  wish we had more time to talk but we'll  talk to you next time and thank you  again for all the information  coming up on law and crime daily the  latest move to the deadly trial  of one of the ex-cops charged in the  george floyd case  plus why dna has answered some questions  in a texas cold case but also created  new ones  part of a 55 year old cold case in texas  has been solved thanks to dna  but the case is far from closed angela  levy tells us why it's still  a mystery investigators know that jolane  hemi drowned in a swimming pool in july  of 1966.  what they don't know is whether or not  her death was an accident  or a homicide we are very pleased  after 55 years to be able to identify  our jane doe is  jolane hammy for decades jolane hemi was  only known as  unknown ex-girl in pecos texas on july  5th  1966 the 17 year old drowned in a motel  swimming pool  she was there with a man who went to the  front desk asking for his registration  card  claiming he needed it as identification  to go to the hospital  but he never showed a facial  reconstruction was conducted which  yielded no clues  but last year jolane was exhumed dna was  collected and testing linked through  genealogy  revealed her true identity and talking  to the family  last week was like talking to him 50  years ago  the emotion was just as raw and just as  solid  of their missing family member we stand  here today  having accomplished the sole purpose of  innovative forensic which is providing  answers to families  it's been a sad story but it's also  with uh we feel a happy ending uh  so we can bring her home and and  have a closure on what happened to her  we  always were afraid that you know  something happened to her and  of course never knowing what  was always kind of a bad deal jolene  hemi's sister told detectives that there  was a man who was interested  in her who kept stopping by the  restaurant where they worked in kansas  city  his name may have been joe investigators  are also eager to determine the name of  the man  who was with joe lane at the motel that  day and they are optimistic that they  can do it  for law and crime daily i'm angenette  levy  an iowa farmer is hoping for a second  chance in civil court after his win  in criminal court law and crime chief  investigative correspondent brian ross  has the latest on the father verse son  saga  thanks brian coming up this week i'm  brian ross investigates the story of a  family tragedy in iowa  the iowa supreme court this week will  hear the case of jason carter  who was accused by his father bill  carter of killing his mother  shirley carter jason carter was found  not guilty in a criminal trial  but he was found guilty in a civil trial  now his lawyers will ask for a new trial  from the iowa supreme court  i talked with jason carter and his wife  shelly about what they expect and what  they want  uh we're hoping that uh they'll either  they'll either throw it out  or at least overturn the the ruling  for the civil and give us a new trial  you know our main hopes are just to have  it thrown out all together but  at the least get a new trial that's  coming up this week on brian ross  investigates on the long crime trial  network brian  thanks brian when we come back a hearing  for the ex-cop charge in the brianna  taylor case  we break down the defense's motion after  the break  prosecutors in the george floyd case are  trying yet again to delay the trial  of one of the former officers charged  with murder  the trial for derek shogun is scheduled  to begin in march a minnesota judge  ordered children be tried separately and  before the three other ex-cops  prosecutors are asking the judge to  reconsider that decision  citing health concerns during the coven  19 pandemic  and are asking for the trials to be  rejoined shoving is also facing tax  evasion charges  that trial has been delayed until june  30th  and in kentucky defense attorneys for  the former detective charge in the  brianna taylor case  are seeking to move his trial brett  hankinson is facing three felony charges  of wanton endangerment for shooting into  a nearby apartment during a raid on  taylor's home last march  no one is charged with taylor's death a  hearing on the motion for a change of  venue is scheduled for march 25th  hankinson's attorney appeared at a  remote hearing via phone  on his behalf and said he plans to file  the motion in about two weeks  i don't have a specific county but i  believe that the statute says it should  be  an adjacent county unless that  is not feasible also and then i assume  any county in the state  is is open i'd love to have it up here  in northern kentucky  well i can understand why you would  prefer that mr matthews  when i was a trial attorney i had a case  that was  the venue was changed now we did not go  to an adjacent county  because of media coverage that you know  would have affected  adjacent counties to jefferson and we  ended up trying the case  in boyle county in the danville  circuit court that gives me some idea of  where you're  thinking aside from possible change in  venue  both sides indicated they're ready for  trial the judge scheduled to begin  on august 31st the discovery has been  provided i don't have any questions  about it and i don't think there are any  um outstanding procedural issues at this  point  and venezuela could you possibly if you  could give me an idea  of how long you think the prosecution's  case would take to try  excluding jury selection i would  anticipate  three to four days for the  commonwealth's case  all right august 31st uh 10 o'clock  to start the jury trial  my panel is back with me for a legal  analysis gg what do you make of this  argument that there should be a change  of venue  is it strategy or is it something else  it's definitely strategy  and for hankerson to succeed on it he's  going to have to show  that he is not likely to get a fair  trial in louisville  due to the massive media attention the  death of brianna taylor  has consistently gotten since early last  year it's going to be really difficult  to find a jury  anywhere that hasn't formed some kind of  opinion on this case  so he's got an uphill battle about uh  getting a  change of venue here and terry we heard  it could be a potentially very short  trial what do you think that trial looks  like and could it spark  more questions as to why no one's being  charged for brianna taylor's death  i do think it will be a short trial it's  only one officer i think they're going  to argue that hankinson was under the  belief  that the information and the search  warrant was accurate and that he  was acting reasonably under the  circumstances  and shooting into the apartment was not  unreasonable  gg terry thank you very much and thank  you for joining us here on law and crime  daily  we'll see you next time as we discuss  justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

